Aerie-Purpose: Give an audio worklet its page's noise tokens and noise the samples that an AudioWorkletProcessor or a ScriptProcessorNode reads, with the same per-site audio noise as a rendered buffer, so the standard audio fingerprint cannot be read raw through a worklet.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webaudio/audio_worklet_processor.cc, script_processor_node.cc and audio_worklet_messaging_proxy.h, base_audio_context.h; third_party/blink/renderer/core/workers/threaded_worklet_messaging_proxy.cc and .h, worklet_global_scope.cc
Aerie-Kind: hook

Issue #157, found by the M1 security sweep. Helium 0.22 noises audio where it leaves the graph: the
rendered buffer of an OfflineAudioContext (AudioBuffer::JitterAudioData) and AnalyserNode reads, both through
AudioReadbackNoise. An AudioWorkletNode at the end of the same graph got the raw samples, and could post them
to the page, so the audio fingerprint came out the same on every site.

The messaging proxy of an audio worklet copies the noise tokens of the creating window into the creation
params (the map is empty while protection is off for the site or globally), and WorkletGlobalScope puts them
on the global scope. Other threaded worklets read nothing that is noised and keep an empty map.
AudioWorkletProcessor::Process then runs AudioReadbackNoise on the copied input arrays, after the copy and
before process(): the seed is the kAudio token plus every sample of that block over all channels, and each
sample is jittered by its position in the block. The algorithm is Helium's, unchanged. A worklet sees only
one 128-frame block at a time, so its values cannot equal those of the whole rendered buffer (that hash covers
samples that are not rendered yet); they follow the same rules: stable for one site and signal, different
per site, within the same +/-0.01% bound, and no counter.

ScriptProcessorNode noises the copy of the input buffer that it hands to onaudioprocess, the same way, and
leaves the backing buffer that the graph writes alone.
One helper in base_audio_context.h, JitterReadbackChannels, does the hashing and the jittering for both readers,
so they cannot drift apart.

A side effect: a worklet that passes its noised input on to its output changes what the rendered buffer of an
OfflineAudioContext shows, because the buffer then gets the input noise and its own noise on top. MediaStreamAudioDestinationNode is not changed: its
samples leave as a MediaStream track that many consumers read, which is a known gap (controls.json).

The source gate audio_worklet_noise (tools/aerie_build/treegate.py) fails if any part goes missing.

